<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Mingrelia</h1><p class="st-meta">1557 CE – 1867 CE · capital Zugdidi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A western Georgian principality ruled by the Dadiani princes, which under Levan II dominated western Georgia and kept autonomy until 1867.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mingrelia, or Odishi, the lowland between the Rioni and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enguri">Inguri</a>, had been governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-dadiani">Dadiani</a> family as eristavis since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti</a> weakened, the Dadiani became effectively independent, and from 1557 they ruled as sovereign princes, paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The princes ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zugdidi">Zugdidi</a> and other residences over nobles and a largely enserfed peasantry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levan-ii-dadiani">Levan II Dadiani</a>, from 1611, made Mingrelia the leading power of western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a>, fighting <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Imereti</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-abkhazia">Abkhazia</a>; Catholic missionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcangelo-lamberti">Arcangelo Lamberti</a> described his court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> with Ottoman ports was a lasting curse. The Mingrelian language was spoken alongside Georgian.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigol-dadiani">Grigol Dadiani</a> accepted a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1803. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> and a peasant revolt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> took over the administration in 1857 and formally annexed the principality in 1867. Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekaterine-dadiani">Ekaterine Dadiani</a> was the last ruling figure.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-mingrelia.jpg" alt="Principality of Mingrelia" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Havsjö · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_The_Principality_of_Mingrelia_(Portolan_1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1557 CE – 1867 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zugdidi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Levan II Dadiani, Grigol Dadiani, Ekaterine Dadiani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mingrelian; Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mingrelia, Odishi, Samegrelo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1712&amp;focus=principality-of-mingrelia" aria-label="Principality of Mingrelia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5694 666 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Mingrelia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6014 922L6036 930M6014 922L5709 970M6014 922L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6036 930h0M5709 970h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6014 922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6014 922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zugdidi. <a href="/atlas?year=1712&amp;focus=principality-of-mingrelia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
